Post-Infectious Cough
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Dry/tickling non-productive cough | Clear temporal onset following acute URTI | Duration of 3-8 weeks with normal chest X-ray

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-017
Reason for Visit: Acute paroxysms of severe nocturnal coughing causing mild chest pain and shortness of breath, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude atypical pneumonia or acute pulmonary embolism.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate severe, uncontrollable coughing associated with chest discomfort. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out evolving lower respiratory tract infection or reactive airway crisis, preventing respiratory compromise prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I cannot stop coughing, my throat is constantly tickling, and my ribs feel like they are bruised from heaving so hard.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a persistent cough lasting four weeks after an initial mild viral respiratory infection. There is a relentless dry, tickling sensation in the throat and chest. The patient notes severe cough exacerbation upon exposure to cold air or strong odors, and a distinct worsening of the cough at night or upon lying down. There is mild chest wall soreness from repeated coughing paroxysms. Initial viral symptoms like fever or myalgia have completely cleared. There is an absolute absence of purulent sputum. The patient complains of significant hoarseness secondary to vocal cord irritation.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but exhausted by sleep disruption.
Social/Safety Concerns: None.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 mmHg | HR: 86 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued and experiences frequent, dry coughing fits during the interview but is not in acute respiratory distress. A brief orthostatic check incidentally reveals a sensation of impending faintness, with relief upon lying flat and an absence of true syncope (a separate, mild positional symptom).
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals normal lung auscultation with equal air entry bilaterally. There are no wheezes, crackles, or rhonchi. Occasional scattered clearings are noted on forced expiration. Chest wall is mildly tender to palpation bilaterally over the costochondral junctions, consistent with mechanical strain. Cardiovascular exam is completely normal.
Environmental Observations: A strong scent of bleach is noted in the bathroom adjacent to the bedroom, likely acting as a chemical trigger.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter antitussives with no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Infectious Cough - Status: Severe mechanical exacerbation secondary to airway hyperreactivity.
Differentials:

Atypical Pneumonia - Ruled out definitively due to the complete absence of fever, clear lung auscultation, and lack of purulent sputum production.

Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out due to the absence of wheeze on forced expiration, normal oxygen saturations, and lack of true dyspnea between coughing fits.

Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) - Ruled out as the primary etiology due to the strict temporal relationship to a viral infection and absence of classic heartburn or regurgitation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient required immediate after-hours assessment due to severe, unrelenting coughing paroxysms causing sleep deprivation, chest pain, and patient fear of a serious lung infection. Telephone triage appropriately identified the risk of acute pneumonia or a severe asthmatic crisis requiring acute intervention. Thorough physical examination confirmed clear lung fields, normal oxygenation, and mechanical (non-cardiac) chest wall pain. By conducting this assessment, the acute fear of serious cardiopulmonary pathology was safely eliminated, avoiding an unnecessary emergency department visit and inappropriate empiric antibiotic prescription, whilst securing the patient with effective symptomatic strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommended specialized nighttime cough suppressants and strict avoidance of aero-irritants. The expected investigation results include a normal chest X-ray and absence of active infection markers, which would assist in confirming the diagnosis via outpatient workup if symptoms persist beyond 8 weeks.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce airway hyperreactivity at night, elevate the head of your bed by sleeping on two thick pillows and run a cool-mist humidifier; physically altering the airway angle and moisture level will directly suppress the mechanical tickle receptor in your throat.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one week to assess resolution or need for an inhaled corticosteroid trial.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a new high fever, begins coughing up thick green phlegm or blood, or experiences severe shortness of breath at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; chronic coughs are multifactorial and require re-evaluation if they persist longer than eight weeks.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves a very slow, frustrating resolution over several weeks as the denuded respiratory epithelium gradually regenerates.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you develop stabbing chest pain that stops you from breathing in, or if you begin to produce significant amounts of blood when you cough.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Antibiotics are strictly contraindicated tonight because your infection is entirely gone; your cough is caused by exposed nerve endings in your throat that are hypersensitive to air movement.
Actionable Steps: Dissolve a spoonful of honey in warm water before bed to naturally coat the throat, and avoid speaking loudly or excessively to reduce mechanical vocal cord trauma.

